course: dictionary
lesson: 13435
title: Hopefully: two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Hopefully» can mean "if things go well", or it can mean "in a hopeful way". The words around it show you which one. «Hopefully it won't rain tomorrow» is about the future: the speaker wishes for good weather.
explain: «The dog looked at me hopefully.» Here «hopefully» describes how the dog looked. The dog was full of hope, wishing for a treat. Ask yourself: is the word talking about the future, or about how someone feels or acts?
explain: Use the swap test. Put "I hope that" in place of the word: "I hope that it won't rain tomorrow" still makes sense, so it is the first meaning. Put "with hope" in place of the word: "The dog looked at me with hope" makes sense, so it is the second meaning.
fact: «Hopefully» is an adverb, a word that tells you more about a verb, an adjective or a whole sentence.
fact: «Hopefully» can mean "if things go well", as in «Hopefully the bus will be on time.»
fact: «Hopefully» can also mean "in a hopeful way", as in «She waited hopefully by the door.»
fact: You may hear phrases like «hopefully not» and «hopefully soon».
fact: Swap test: put "I hope that" or "with hope" into the sentence to check which meaning fits.
exercise: «Hopefully the bus will be on time.» What does hopefully mean here? | *If things go well, it is hoped that | Very quickly | In a sad and tired way | Very angrily
exercise: «The little girl waited hopefully by the door for her dad.» What does hopefully describe? | The dad, who was late | *How she waited, full of hope | The weather outside | The door, which was shut
exercise: Pick the sentence where hopefully means "if things go well". | He opened the box hopefully. | She smiled hopefully at her teacher. | The cat sat hopefully by its bowl. | *Hopefully we will win the match on Friday.
test: «Hopefully soon the rain will stop.» Which phrase could replace hopefully here? | Sadly | *I hope that | Angrily
test: «Nan watched the cake in the oven hopefully.» What does hopefully mean? | Burnt and smelly | *In a hopeful way, wishing for good results | Without any oven | Very slowly and quietly
exam: «Hopefully we can go to the park, but I am not sure yet.» What does hopefully mean? | *If things go well, it is hoped that | In an angry way | Hungrily | Very, very loudly
